# Badges F–G: checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.fire-safety
@note The current official page and older copies worded and numbered these requirements differently, so no requirement outline is published yet. Topics include fire science, extinguishers, burns and carbon monoxide, home fire safety and escape plans, flammable liquids, public buildings, campfires, wildland fires and careers.
@desc Fire Safety teaches how fire behaves and how to prevent it: the fire tetrahedron, extinguishers and fire classes, burn and clothing-fire first aid, home escape plans and inspections, safe fuels and campfires, wildland fire prevention, and careers in fire safety.
@safety Practice fire skills only with adult supervision and in approved fire areas; never fight a fire that is spreading. Get out and call for help.

@owner mb.fish-wildlife-management
@complete complete
@desc Fish & Wildlife Management looks at how wildlife and fisheries are conserved: the problems they face, how the public helps, management practices in your state, a hands-on habitat project, wildlife observation and records, studying fish, and careers in conservation.
1 k Conservation and management :: The goals of fish and wildlife conservation and management.
2 k Problems facing wildlife :: Major problems facing fish and wildlife resources in your state.
3 k How everyone can help :: Ways the public can contribute to conservation.
4 k Management practices :: Management practices used by your state’s fish and wildlife managers.
5 a A habitat project :: One option, such as nest boxes, bird feeders, a fishery or backyard habitat project, or a wildlife blind.
6 a Wildlife records :: One option: observing and logging species, studying your state’s species of concern, or a wildlife scrapbook.
7 a Studying fish :: One option, such as aging fish from scales, a creel census, or keeping an aquarium.
8 a Careers :: Careers in fish and wildlife management and researching one of them.

@owner mb.fishing
@note Excerpts showed the topics (health and safety, equipment, knots, lures and baits, regulations, conservation and ethics, and catching and cooking a fish) but not their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Fishing covers safe, ethical angling: hazards and first aid, rods and reels, essential fishing knots, lures and natural baits, regulations, Leave No Trace and catch and release, and catching, identifying and cooking a fish.
@safety Wear a life jacket when fishing from a boat, know how to remove a hook safely, and follow local fishing regulations.

@owner mb.fly-fishing
@complete complete
@desc Fly Fishing teaches the craft of fishing with flies: safety, a balanced rod, line and leader, knots, the kinds of flies and tying your own, casting, reading what fish eat, catch and release, regulations and sportsmanship, and catching and cooking a fish.
@safety Wear eye protection while casting and a life jacket on boats; know how to remove a hook safely.
1 k! Health and safety :: Likely hazards, safety practices, and preventing and treating common problems such as cuts, hypothermia and sunburn.
2 k A balanced system :: Matching rod, line and leader, types of fly line, and caring for your gear.
3 demo Knots :: The knots that join backing, line, leader, tippet and fly.
4 m Flies :: The main kinds of flies, what they imitate, and tying flies of your own.
5 demo Casting :: Overhead and roll casts at a set distance.
6 k Observing fish :: What fish eat above and below the surface, and matching the hatch.
7 k Catch and release :: What catch and release means and how to release a fish safely.
8 k Regulations :: Local fishing regulations and why they exist.
9 d Sportsmanship :: Outdoor ethics for anglers, including Leave No Trace and the Outdoor Code.
10 a Catching a fish :: Catching a fish on a fly and identifying it.
11 a Cleaning and cooking :: Where regulations allow, cleaning and cooking a fish.

@owner mb.forestry
@complete partial
@note Excerpts listed the topics in order with approximate numbering; confirm the numbering against the official page. The 2026 update revised the managed-forest and logging-visit items.
@desc Forestry explores forests as living systems and working landscapes: identifying trees, wood and growth rings, what forests give us, sustainable management, field visits with foresters, tree hazards, forest threats, and wildfire.
1 a Identifying trees :: Identifying local trees, shrubs and vines and their features, habitats and uses.
2 a Wood or growth rings :: One option: collecting wood samples, or studying growth rings in stumps, logs or cores.
3 k Benefits of forests :: How forests contribute to the economy, recreation, soil, water, air and wildlife.
4 k Forest management :: Multiple-use and sustainable management and common cutting systems.
5 a Field visits :: Visiting a managed forest and a logging or mill operation, and fire prevention.
6 a Tree hazards :: An inventory of hazardous trees, or reviewing a professional hazard report.
7 k! Forest threats and wildfire :: Threats such as wildfire, insects and overgrazing, and what to do if you find a forest fire.

@owner mb.game-design
@complete complete
@desc Game Design teaches how games are made: analyzing games and play value, design terms, intellectual property, experimenting with rules, designing and prototyping your own game, playtesting with real players, and meeting game development professionals.
1 a Analyzing games :: Analyzing games from different media and the kinds of play value they offer.
2 k Design terms :: Game design terms and how they apply to specific games.
3 k Intellectual property :: What intellectual property is, how it is protected, and licensed properties in games.
4 a Changing the rules :: Playing a game, then testing and comparing rule changes.
5 a Designing a game :: A vision statement, preliminary rules and drawings in a design notebook.
6 a Prototype and playtest :: Building a prototype, playtesting and revising the rules.
7 a Instructions and feedback :: Writing instructions, watching new players use them, and reviewing their feedback.
8 a Meeting a professional :: One option: visiting a game development professional or educator.

@owner mb.gardening
@complete complete
@desc Gardening teaches how to grow food and flowers: garden safety, raising vegetables and flowers from seed and seedlings, nutrition, germination, pollinators, pests, a hands-on project such as composting or hydroponics, and gardening careers or hobbies.
@safety Use gardening tools carefully and handle fertilizers and pesticides only with adult supervision, following label directions.
1 k! Safety :: Garden hazards and preventing or treating related health problems, including pesticide exposure.
2 a Growing plants :: Raising vegetables and flowers from seeds and seedlings.
3 k Nutrition :: The nutritional value of root crops, above-ground vegetables and fruits.
4 a Germination :: Testing seeds and working out the germination rate.
5 a A visit :: Visiting an extension office, nursery, farm or garden and reporting on it.
6 k Pollinators :: How honeybees pollinate crops and the threats bees face.
7 k Pests :: Garden pests and solutions for each, including organic ones.
8 a A gardening project :: One option, such as a compost or worm bin, hydroponics, a water garden or your own garden.
9 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: a gardening career, or a gardening hobby or healthy lifestyle.

@owner mb.genealogy
@note Excerpts showed only part of the 2025 list (terms, a timeline or journal, a family interview, records and resources, research organizations, and family charts), so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Genealogy is the study of family history: key terms, timelines and journals, interviewing relatives, finding records such as census, birth and marriage records, research organizations, and building a family tree.
